#!/usr/bin/env -S uv run --script
# /// script
# requires-python = ">=3.11"
# dependencies = [
#   "docling>=2.0",
#   "gliner>=0.2.13",
#   "torch",
#   "transformers",
#   "typer>=0.12",
# ]
# ///
"""Redact PII from PDFs into Markdown.

Pipeline (per file):
  1. Convert PDF → Docling document.
  2. Per-text-item: collect spans from regex pre-patterns + GLiNER over the
     ORIGINAL text; resolve overlaps with span-splitting (no whitespace-search,
     no dropped non-overlapping pieces); apply replacements end-to-start.
  3. Render markdown via `doc.export_to_markdown()`.
  4. Post-pass over the rendered markdown: re-run regex pre-patterns (catches
     IDs in Docling-synthesized links/annotations the redactor never saw) AND
     sweep for literal recurrences of every entity_map key (catches names that
     appear elsewhere in the doc but GLiNER missed on the second occurrence).
  5. Write `<stem>.md` (0644) next to the input PDF, plus a dot-prefixed
     sibling `.<stem>.entity_map.json` (0600) holding the original surfaces
     for any de-anonymization. Override location with `--out-dir`.

LANGUAGES
  Selftest + real documents:         DE, EN (author's private corpus)
  Selftest only:                     FR, ES
  Regex anchors only, no selftest:   IT, PT
  Other:                             GLiNER still handles names/emails/IBANs
                                     language-agnostically, but the
                                     language-specific regex anchors (DOB
                                     labels, role nouns, customer-ID labels)
                                     won't fire. A WARN is emitted if the
                                     detected language falls outside the
                                     supported set.

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
NOTE TO AGENTS / LLMs (consumer mode)
  This tool exists so the original PDF stays away from the LLM. If you are
  an agent using the redacted output for downstream work (analysis,
  summarization, comparison, drafting):
    1. Run this tool first.
    2. Read ONLY the resulting `<stem>.md`.
    3. Do NOT open the source `.pdf` (Read tool, pdftotext, OCR, etc.).
    4. Do NOT read the `.<stem>.entity_map.json` sibling: it contains the
       original surfaces and re-identifies the document.

  Auditor / debugger mode: if you are working ON this redactor itself
  (verifying false negatives, tuning thresholds, adding rules), you DO need
  to read source PDFs to compare against output. Do that on a workspace
  not shared with other agents.
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

USAGE
  redact-pdf input.pdf                          # → ./input.md (next to PDF)
  redact-pdf *.pdf -j 4                         # batch, 4 parallel workers
  redact-pdf input.pdf -o ~/redacted            # explicit output dir
  redact-pdf input.pdf --strict                 # also redact orgs/URLs/BIC/VAT
  redact-pdf input.pdf --rename ask             # prompt for clean output name
  redact-pdf input.pdf --rename "vertrag-2026"  # explicit output stem
  redact-pdf input.pdf --json                   # machine-readable result lines
  redact-pdf --selftest                         # built-in correctness check

FILENAME PII
  If the input filename contains PII (a name, address, structured ID, …) it
  leaks into the output filename. Default is `--rename keep` (use slug of
  stem) + a WARN line on stderr. Override with:
    --rename keep        # slug of input stem (default; warn on PII)
    --rename auto        # derive from doc title/heading; fallback to content hash
    --rename ask         # interactive prompt (TTY only)
    --rename NAME        # explicit stem (single file only)
    --rename hash        # 8-char hash of file CONTENT (anonymous, deterministic)

EXIT CODES
  0  all files ok
  2  bad invocation (Typer default)
  3  one or more files failed (incl. redaction errors: output suppressed)
  4  no input files found
  5  selftest failure
"""
